Built and verified locally. Here's what's in the project and what you need to paste into Vercel.

## What it does

`/api/cron` runs daily at 09:00 UTC. Each run:

1. Asks OpenRouter for a post **plus** an image prompt (single JSON reply).
2. Sends that prompt to your image worker (the `worker_url` from `automation.py`, now `IMAGE_API_URL`).
3. Refreshes a LinkedIn access token from your refresh token.
4. `POST /rest/images?action=initializeUpload` → `PUT` the PNG bytes.
5. `POST /rest/posts` with text + image.

The `automation.py` logic is ported to [lib/image.js](lib/image.js) and accepts raw image bytes, `{b64_json}`, or `{url}` responses. Posting uses the current versioned LinkedIn API (the old `/v2/ugcPosts` is legacy), and commentary is backslash-escaped because LinkedIn silently truncates posts at unescaped `( ) [ ] { } < > @ | ~ _ *`.

## Env names to add in Vercel

**Required (7):**
```
CRON_SECRET
OPENROUTER_API_KEY
IMAGE_API_URL
IMAGE_API_KEY
LINKEDIN_CLIENT_ID
LINKEDIN_CLIENT_SECRET
LINKEDIN_REFRESH_TOKEN        # or LINKEDIN_ACCESS_TOKEN if LinkedIn won't issue one
```

**Optional / recommended (9):**
```
OPENROUTER_MODEL              # e.g. google/gemini-2.5-flash (default)
LINKEDIN_PERSON_URN           # urn:li:person:…, saves a lookup per run
LINKEDIN_API_VERSION          # default 202510; bump when LinkedIn sunsets it
POST_TOPIC
POST_TONE
POST_AUDIENCE
POST_WITH_IMAGE               # false = text-only post
PUBLIC_BASE_URL               # https://<project>.vercel.app
LINKEDIN_REDIRECT_URI         # only if you need an exact override
```

All 16 names are already in [.env.example](.env.example), so you can fill that in and upload it to Vercel directly. [.gitignore](.gitignore) ignores `.env` and `.env.*` (except `.env.example`), so nothing secret gets committed.

## Order of operations

1. `vercel` then `vercel --prod` (no dependencies, no build step).
2. In your LinkedIn app, add the redirect URL exactly: `https://<project>.vercel.app/api/auth/callback`, and request **Share on LinkedIn** (`w_member_social`) + **Sign In with LinkedIn using OpenID Connect**.
3. Add the env vars in Vercel and redeploy.
4. Open `/`, click **Connect LinkedIn**, copy `LINKEDIN_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `LINKEDIN_REFRESH_TOKEN`, `LINKEDIN_PERSON_URN` into Vercel.
5. Dry-run it: `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" "https://<project>.vercel.app/api/cron?dry=1"` — generates text + image, posts nothing.

## Verified / not verified

- 12 tests pass (`npm test`, network mocked): cron auth, missing-env errors, dry run, refresh-token publishing, static-token fallback, text-only posts, base64 images, version pinning, and all four failure paths.
- Ran the real HTTP routes locally: OAuth start returns 302 with the right scopes and state cookie, the callback page renders the tokens, cron returns 401 without the secret.
- Not verified: live calls to OpenRouter, your image worker, or LinkedIn (no keys, and deploying needs your Vercel account), and no deployment was made.

Three things to know: LinkedIn only issues a refresh token if **Programmatic Refresh Tokens** is enabled on the app (otherwise re-auth every ~60 days); Hobby plans run cron once per day; and `api/cron.js` is capped at 60s in [vercel.json](vercel.json) — raise it if your image worker is slow.

Full setup detail is in [README.md](README.md), with the flow in [lib/pipeline.js](lib/pipeline.js) and LinkedIn specifics in [lib/linkedin.js](lib/linkedin.js).
